div.p:
They also plundered one another on the mainland. And to this day much of Hellas lives in the old
              way, around the Ozolian Locrians and the Aetolians and the Acarnanians and the mainland
              there. And the carrying of iron weapons has remained among these mainland peoples from the old piracy;

and the people by the sea, now acquiring wealth more, lived more
              securely, and some even put walls around themselves as they became richer than before;
              for in their desire for gain the weaker accepted the slavery of the stronger,
              and the more powerful, having surplus wealth, made the smaller cities their
              subjects.

But because of the lack of money the events before these were weak,
              and these very events, though the most famous of those before, are shown by the facts to be inferior
              to their fame and to the account of them which now prevails because of the poets;

But when this did not succeed for him and the money was being spent in vain, Megabazus with the rest of
              the money was brought back again to Asia, and he sent Megabyzus son of Zopyrus, a Persian, with a large
              army;

But when Pericles came back they were shut in again by the ships. And later there came as further help
              from Athens forty ships with Thucydides and Hagnon and Phormio, and twenty with Tlepolemus and Anticles,
              and thirty from Chios and Lesbos.

And the King, as is said, admired his purpose and told him to do so. And
              in the time that he waited he learned as much as he could of the Persian language and
              of the customs of the country;

For this small thing holds the entire confirmation and test of your resolve. If you
              give in to them, you will at once be ordered to do something greater, as having obeyed this too out of fear;
              but if you refuse firmly, you would make it clear to them that they must deal with you rather on equal terms.

And such people cannot often man fleets or send out land armies,
              since they are at the same time away from their own property and spending from their own means, and besides are also
              shut off from the sea;

and if they build a fort, they might harm part of our land by raids and
              desertions, but it will certainly not be enough to prevent us from sailing to their land and building forts there
              and from retaliating with our ships, in which we are strong;

Of these, a navy was provided by the Corinthians, Megarians, Sicyonians, Pellenians, Eleans,
              Ambraciots, and Leucadians, and cavalry by the Boeotians, Phocians, and Locrians; and the other cities provided infantry.
              This was the alliance of the Lacedaemonians;

And many also set themselves up in the towers of the walls and wherever each could;
              for the city could not hold them when they had all come together, but later they occupied the long walls,
              dividing them up, and most of the Piraeus.

And the army of the Peloponnesians, advancing, came first to Oenoe
              in Attica, where they intended to invade. And when they had encamped, they prepared to
              make assaults on the wall with siege engines and in other ways;

And Atalanta, too, was fortified by the Athenians as a fort at the
              end of this summer, the island off the Opuntian Locrians, which had previously been uninhabited,
              so that pirates sailing out from Opus and the rest of Locris should not damage Euboea.

and when the funeral procession takes place, wagons carry coffins of cypress wood, one for each tribe;
              and in each are the bones of those of that tribe. And one empty bier is carried, spread, for the missing,
              those who could not be found for recovery.

But those of you who are still of an age to have children must bear up in the hope of other children;
              for in private life those who are born later will make some forget those who are no more, and it will benefit the city in two ways,
              both by its not being left empty and by its security; for it is not possible for those to deliberate fairly or justly
              who do not also have children to stake and risk on equal terms.

So every form of wickedness arose in the Greek world because of
              the civil strife, and simplicity, of which nobility has the largest share, was laughed at and disappeared, while
              mutual opposition of feeling, full of distrust, prevailed far and wide;

for there was nothing to reconcile them, neither a binding word nor a fearful oath, but all, when they were stronger,
              reckoning that security was hopeless, took precautions against being harmed rather than being able
              to trust.

So the Corcyraeans in the city indulged in such first outbursts of passion
              against one another, and Eurymedon and the Athenians sailed away with their
              ships;

I myself think the cause of such a thing is that, where the earthquake was strongest, at that point
              it drives back the sea and, suddenly drawing it back again, makes the flood more violent;
              but without an earthquake it does not seem to me that such a thing could have happened.

When they had heard them, the Lacedaemonians resolved to send out the colony, wishing to help both the
              Trachinians and the Dorians, and at the same time the city seemed to them well placed for the war against the Athenians;
              for a fleet could be prepared there against Euboea, so that the crossing would be short,
              and it would be useful for the route to Thrace. In short, they were eager to found the place.

The Aetolians, throwing javelins, caught many of them there in the rout at their heels,
              being swift-footed and light-armed men, and killed them, and the greater number, who missed the roads and went into
              the forest, from which there were no ways out, they burned up, bringing fire round them;

for Demosthenes had purposely placed the Messenians in front and ordered them to address the enemy,
              speaking the Doric dialect and so gaining the trust of the outposts, while at the same time they could
              not be distinguished by sight, as it was still night.

The Ambraciots, then, having suffered in this way, came safely back to their city, few out of many;
              and the Acarnanians, after stripping the dead and setting up trophies, withdrew to
              Argos.

Having agreed on these terms, they ended the war. After this the Corinthians sent a garrison of
              their own to Ambracia, about three hundred hoplites, with Xenocleidas son of Euthycles as commander;
              these made their way with difficulty across the mainland and arrived. Such were the events at Ambracia.

The greater part, then, both of the unarmed and of the armed, he posted at the most fortified and
              strongest points of the place toward the mainland, ordering them to ward off the infantry if it attacked;
              but he himself, picking out from all of them sixty hoplites and a few archers, went outside the
              wall to the sea, where he most expected the enemy to try to land, to ground that was
              difficult and rocky, facing the open sea, but where, since their wall was weakest at this
              point, he thought the enemy would be eager to force a landing;

And the exiles of the Mytilenaeans and of the other Lesbians, most of them setting out from the mainland,
              and having hired mercenaries from the Peloponnese and gathered others there, took
              Rhoeteum, and after receiving two thousand Phocaean staters they gave it back again without doing any harm;

For negotiations about Boeotian affairs were being carried on with him and Hippocrates by certain men in the cities,
              who wished to change the constitution and turn it into a democracy like the Athenians;
              and these arrangements were made for them chiefly at the instigation of Ptoeodorus, an exile from Thebes.

For Arrhabaeus too had made some overtures by herald, being ready to submit to Brasidas as an impartial judge;
              and the envoys of the Chalcidians who were present kept advising him not to remove the dangers from Perdiccas' path,
              so that they might find him more eager to serve their own interests too.

‘The sending out of me and the army by the Lacedaemonians,
              Acanthians, has taken place to confirm the reason which we declared at the beginning of the war,
              that we would make war on the Athenians to free Greece;

For we Lacedaemonians, thinking that we were coming to allies, who even before we actually arrived
              would be with us at least in spirit and would welcome us, have run so great a risk, marching through
              foreign territory a journey of many days and showing every zeal;

And it happened that, when Pagondas had sent two squadrons of cavalry round the hill out of sight,
              since their left was in distress, and these suddenly appeared over it, the victorious wing of the Athenians,
              thinking that another army was attacking, fell into a panic;

for it was the established custom of all, when invading each other's land, to keep away from the sanctuaries in it,
              whereas the Athenians had fortified Delium and were living in it, and everything that men do in an unconsecrated place
              was being done there, and they were drawing up and using the water, which was untouchable for them except for use as
              lustral water for the sacrifices;

After this Thucydides set matters in order at Eion, so that it would be safe
              both for the present, if Brasidas attacked, and for the future, receiving those who wished
              to come there from upstream according to the treaty;

Its cities are Sane, a colony of the Andrians, right by the canal, facing the sea towards Euboea,
              and the others Thyssus and Cleonae and Acrothoi and Olophyxus and
              Dium;

‘Resolved by the people. Acamantis held the prytany, Phaenippus was secretary, Niciades presided.
              Laches moved, with good fortune for the Athenians, to make the truce on the terms that the
              Lacedaemonians and their allies grant and agreed to before the people;

Those who concluded and poured libations were, for the Lacedaemonians, the following: Taurus son of Echetimidas, Athenaeus
              son of Pericleidas, Philocharidas son of Eryxilaidas; for the Corinthians, Aeneas son of Ocytus, Euphamidas son of Aristonymus;
              for the Sicyonians, Damotimus son of Naucrates, Onasimus son of Megacles; for the Megarians, Nicasus son of Cecalus,
              Menecrates son of Amphidorus; for the Epidaurians, Amphias son of Eupaidas; for the Athenians, the generals
              Nicostratus son of Diitrephes, Nicias son of Niceratus, Autocles son of Tolmaeus’.

‘Men of the Peloponnese, from what sort of country we have come, that it is
              always free because of its courage, and that you, Dorians, are about to fight Ionians, whom you are accustomed
              to be superior to, let it be enough to have shown briefly;

And whoever, having best seen such mistakes of the enemy and at the same time in accordance with his own
              force, makes his attack not so much openly and in a set battle line as
              according to what is advantageous for the present situation, would succeed most often;

For they accused him of having persuaded the prophetess at Delphi, together with Aristocles his brother,
              to give this oracle to the Lacedaemonians for a long time when they came as envoys: to bring back the seed of the demigod son
              of Zeus from the foreign land to their own, or else they would plough with a silver ploughshare;

For when the fifty-year treaty had been made and
              afterwards the alliance, the embassies from the Peloponnese, which had been summoned for these things,
              withdrew from Lacedaemon;

So they said they were not breaking their oaths to the allies by not entering into the treaty with the Athenians;
              for having sworn pledges by the gods to those men, they would not be keeping their oaths if they betrayed them.
              And it had been stated: ‘unless there is a hindrance from gods or heroes’; and this appeared to them to be a divine hindrance.

Nevertheless the Eleans, fearing that they would sacrifice by force, kept a guard of their younger men under arms;
              and Argives and Mantineans also came to them, a thousand of each, and Athenian horsemen, who
              waited at Harpine for the festival.

And orders, if they want anything, pass in the same way and arrive quickly;
              for almost the whole army of the Lacedaemonians, except for a small part, consists of commanders of commanders,
              and responsibility for what is done falls on many.

And the Samians and the soldiers sent off the ship Paralus, and on it Chaereas son of Archestratus, an Athenian, who had been
             eager for the change of government, with all speed to Athens to report what had happened;
             for they did not yet know that the Four Hundred were in power.

And at the same time (for they had learned in advance that they were eager for a sea-battle from Miletus) they were expecting
              Strombichides from the Hellespont to come to their help with the ships from Chios that had gone to Abydus;
              for a messenger had been sent ahead to him.

At any rate, with whatever intention, Tissaphernes came to Aspendus and met the Phoenicians;
              and the Peloponnesians, at his bidding, sent Philippus, a Lacedaemonian, with two triremes as if
              to fetch the ships.

and when these arrived, together with those already in Euboea, they came to thirty-six. And at once
              they were forced to fight at sea; for Hegesandridas, after his men had eaten their midday meal, led his ships out from Oropus;
              and Oropus is at most sixty stades from the city of the Eretrians by sea.

So Thrasyllus, sailing against it with all his ships, intended to make an assault;
              and Thrasybulus too had arrived there before him with five ships from Samos, when
              this crossing of the exiles had been reported to them; but being too late, he went to Eresus and lay at anchor off it.
